::SQL Data Constraints::
SQL data constraints are used to restrict the values of data which are inserted into a table.
{
    =True
    ~False
}



::SQL Data Constraints::
Given a table with a column named 'id', which is meant to provide a unique identifier for rows, choose the best constraint to apply to the column.
{
    =Unique
    ~Foreign Key
    ~Create Index
    ~None of these
}

::SQL Data Constraints::
Given a table with a column named 'created_at', which is meant to provide a timestamp for insertion time, choose the best constraint to apply to the column.
{
    =Default now()
    ~Not Null
    ~Check
    ~None of these
}

::SQL Data Constraints::
Given a table with a column named 'product_type', which is meant to link this table with data in another table, choose the best constraint to apply to the column.
{
    ~Primary Key
    =Foreign Key
    ~Default
    ~None of these
}

